    {
      "id": "41d7338d-9648-4f26-910a-1563aad7dabb",
      "phaseId": "5464ff74-fa17-4412-abaa-95aa19810b33",
      "number": 416,
      "shortId": "HEQ9R",
      "priority": 5,
      "shortName": "surface-scoped-keybindings-the",
      "title": "Surface-scoped keybindings: the active context owns the keyboard, and hints/menus follow it",
      "status": "done",
      "description": "**Read `docs/surface-compositor.md` §2, §3, §5 and §6 first** — recovered from git 2026-08-12 after being deleted in `3d71092` under a commit message claiming its content had been transferred. This task is that design, applied to input.\n\n# The defect\n\n**While the exposé is open, the app underneath is still being driven.**\n\n- `prefix+j` / `prefix+k` change the focused pane in the session behind the map.\n- **`prefix+/` picks targets that are not on screen.** Antonio, 2026-08-12: *\"prefix+/ acts under the\n  exposé. I don't see the hint but after prefix+/ I type `a` (or a random letter) and see the sidebar\n  changing focus, from the little space around the exposé.\"* The letters are **invisible** — drawn\n  beneath the map or outside it — while the targets are **live**, so a keystroke acts on a surface\n  the user cannot see and has no warning about. Worse than visibly wrong letters.\n- `prefix+p`, `prefix+e` and the rest of the app's bindings all run.\n\nAntonio: *\"In the overlay we should not be able to do a lot of prefix+key things… Each overlay should\ndeclare its own keybinding and actions.\"*\n\n# Why it happens — one flag answering two questions\n\n`docs/surface-compositor.md` §2 keeps **two orthogonal mechanisms** apart:\n\n1. **Coarse — context activation.** `current_index` points at the active context surface. Opening a\n   modal sets it: *\"it's exclusive → everything beneath goes dormant.\"*\n2. **Fine — geometric occlusion.** Within the eligible set, a target is hidden iff a higher-z\n   surface's occluder covers its centre.\n\n`domain_for` (`heca/src/app/interaction.rs:212`) asks the **fine** question to decide the **coarse**\none: it calls `chrome::content_covered(state)` — a fact about what is painted over — to decide\n`Domain::Overlay`. The exposé declares `covers_content: false` because that is geometrically true\n(you can see the panes, and the hint resolver needs that), and by doing so it also, accidentally,\nsays \"the base context is still live\". One flag, two questions, opposite answers needed.\n\n§6's invariant names it: *\"If you find yourself special-casing a surface's hints, the surface is\nmis-modelled — fix the model, don't add a filter.\"*\n\n# What to build\n\n## 1. The coarse gate is \"is a context surface active\", not \"does something cover the content\"\n\n`domain_for` returns `Domain::Overlay` while a **modal** layer is active\n(`LayerRegistry::top_modal_index` exists and already orders by band). Coverage goes back to meaning\nonly what it says. The exposé keeps `covers_content: false` and still makes the base context dormant.\n\n## 2. What survives is `ActionPolicy::Global` — the existing classification\n\n`Domain::Overlay` already permits only `Global`. Two actions must be **reclassified**, being\nsurface-agnostic by nature and acting on whichever context is live:\n\n- `open_context_menu` (`prefix+>`)\n- the leader/hint picker (`prefix+/`)\n\n`reload_config` and `close_overlay` already are. `focus_down`, `command_palette`, `focus_dock` and\nthe rest stay as they are and are refused while a context is up — the wanted behaviour. **`prefix+e`\nis not global**: it is the workspaces component's own `global_focus`\n(`keybindings.default.toml:681`), and it should not work from inside the map.\n\n⚠️ **The map's own intents must keep working**: `close_pane_by_id`, `delete_column`,\n`delete_workspace`, `FocusPaneThenAction` and `CloseOverlay` are dispatched by the exposé's own tree\nwith `InteractionSource::Keyboard`. If the domain rule refuses them the deletes break (only just made\nto work — T327 item 4). Either give a layer's own tree its own `InteractionSource`, **or** judge by\nthe surface the intent came from. Antonio's constraint: *\"it should be valid for all overlays… but\neach overlay might use its own actions and keybindings so we risk blocking future actions.\"* It\ncannot be a fixed allow-list — it has to be the action's own declared policy plus where the intent\ncame from.\n\n## 3. `prefix+/` collects only the active context's targets\n\n`chrome::active_peek_targets` walks the chrome tree, every `state.pane_headers` tree and **every\nvisible layer**. Per §3 the eligible set is *\"`current_index` plus its visible, non-blocking\ndescendants\"* — everything below the active context is suppressed. That filter is missing, which is\nwhy a letter picks a sidebar row while the map is up. Same rule for the context menu.\n\n**Check the drawing too**: even for eligible targets, the keycaps must paint above the active\nsurface. A target whose letter cannot be seen is not pickable in any useful sense — the invisible\nletters above are half of what made this hard to notice.\n\n## 4. Surface-scoped keys: `[[keys.surface]]`\n\nA dock and an overlay are the same thing to the keyboard. `[[keys.component]]`\n(`keybindings.default.toml:676`) already carries the two tiers — `global_focus = \"prefix+e\"` reaches\nthe workspaces dock from outside; `cursor_up` / `activate_selected` / `create_workspace` apply only\nwhile it holds the keyboard. Generalise to **`[[keys.surface]]`** with `name` = the surface id\n(`workspaces`, `expose`, a plugin's), keeping `[[keys.component]]` as an accepted spelling so\nexisting configs keep working. The shape Antonio sketched:\n\n```toml\n[[keys.surface]]\nname         = \"expose\"\nglobal_focus = \"prefix+Tab\"\ndismiss      = \"q,Escape\"\ndelete_pane  = \"x\"\n```\n\nResolution: the surface holding the keyboard first (shadowing, the way a neovim floating window's\nbuffer-local mappings shadow the global map), then the app's, then filtered by the action's policy.\n\nThis also removes today's oddity that the exposé's `x` / `r` / `d` are **literals in Rust**\n(`heca/src/chrome/expose.rs::delete_keys`) rather than bindings a user can rebind.\n\n## 5. A plugin needs nothing host-private\n\n`ActionMeta.policy` already exists (`heca/src/actions.rs:2007`) beside `args`, `confirm` and `icon`,\nand `register_dynamic` registers a name-keyed action. So a plugin declares its actions' policies at\nregistration and its keys as its own surface layer. **Verify this end to end** — it is the Rule Zero\ntest for this task.\n\n**A binding is never \"global\" — the action is.** `[keys]` \"global\" means *typed without the prefix*;\n`ActionPolicy::Global` means *allowed in every domain*. It belongs on the action because the same\naction is reached from a key, a menu entry, the palette, a button and RPC; on the binding, the other\nfour surfaces would have no answer.\n\n# Verification (Antonio drives)\n\nOpen the exposé: `prefix+j/k` do nothing · `prefix+/` shows letters **only on the map's cards**, and\ntyping one acts only there · `prefix+>` opens the map's own menu · `prefix+Shift+r` still reloads ·\n`ctrl+j/k` still move the map's cursor · `x`/`r`/`d` still delete. Then the same with a dock focused,\nto confirm nothing regressed there.",
      "notes": "",
      "statusLog": [
        {
          "id": "41d7338d-9648-4f26-910a-1563aad7dabb-check-001-planned-in-progress",
          "date": "2026-08-11T23:09:17.101Z",
          "fromStatus": "planned",
          "toStatus": "in-progress",
          "title": "Started 2026-08-12 on Antonio's instruction — it is the first item in the P082 handoff. `planner-task-start` recommended P087(F011)/T373 instead on feature-priority order; overridden deliberately, not by accident.",
          "description": "Started 2026-08-12 on Antonio's instruction — it is the first item in the P082 handoff. `planner-task-start` recommended P087(F011)/T373 instead on feature-priority order; overridden deliberately, not by accident."
        },
        {
          "id": "41d7338d-9648-4f26-910a-1563aad7dabb-check-002-in-progress-done",
          "date": "2026-08-12T07:03:09.534Z",
          "fromStatus": "in-progress",
          "toStatus": "done",
          "title": "Landed as 9673be9. Driven and confirmed by Antonio 2026-08-12: \"for now it works good\" — x/r/d delete, prefix+/ shows letters only on the map's cards, prefix+j/k are refused behind it, the startup arg warnings are gone. Whole workspace green, clippy 0 warnings with --all-features.",
          "description": "Landed as 9673be9. Driven and confirmed by Antonio 2026-08-12: \"for now it works good\" — x/r/d delete, prefix+/ shows letters only on the map's cards, prefix+j/k are refused behind it, the startup arg warnings are gone. Whole workspace green, clippy 0 warnings with --all-features.\n\nBuilt: the coarse gate (`base_context_is_dormant`) replaces the `content_covered` question; `InteractionSource::Surface(LayerId)` + one `chrome::layer_emitter` (there were three copies, each claiming a different source); the picker's keycaps paint into the layer scene; the hint stack is assembled front→back by construction rather than sorted by band; `[[keys.surface]]` generalises `[[keys.component]]` with both read through `KeysConfig::surfaces`; the exposé's x/r/d are bindings, read back via `ActionShortcuts::in_surface`; `OpenContextMenu` → Global.\n\nFound on the way and fixed here: `add_named` minted a new LayerId on every rebuild, so anything holding one across a session change was left pointing at a dead layer; and the probe deciding whether to qualify a name reported argument problems about the name it went on to reject, which made the shipped defaults complain about themselves at every startup.\n\nNote for T417: `HintLayer::band` is gone and `LayerRegistry::add_view`/`add_named` carry an `#[allow(clippy::too_many_arguments)]` rather than growing a spec struct, because T417 deletes `LayerBand` and rebuilds these signatures anyway.\n\n`HintPick` needed no reclassification — it was already `ActionPolicy::Global`, which is exactly why prefix+/ fired under the map. The task text expected otherwise."
        }
      ],
