    {
      "id": "dcb1fdd2-065e-4632-a80b-19c1c7835e85",
      "phaseId": "5464ff74-fa17-4412-abaa-95aa19810b33",
      "number": 418,
      "shortId": "4RS7J",
      "priority": 7,
      "shortName": "hovering-a-card-in-the-map-scr",
      "title": "The map's hover behaviour — the autocentring loop, the animation, and a card that is hard to click",
      "status": "in-progress",
      "description": "**Antonio, driving, 2026-08-12 (second report, after T416 landed):** *\"the animation when hovering the cards and the autocentering behavior need deeper review because its strange. mouse hover is hard to click.\"*\n\n**First report, 2026-08-12:** *\"when there is only one workspace in the exposé, moving hover with the mouse causes the map to slide right and left to center the hovered pane, but this brings it too far from the mouse.\"*\n\n⚠️ **This is a REVIEW, not only a fix.** Three symptoms are reported and only the first has a diagnosis. Do not stop at the loop below and declare it done — drive all three and confirm each.\n\n| # | Symptom | Status |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Hovering slides the map to centre the card, moving it away from the pointer | diagnosed, below |\n| 2 | The hover animation itself \"is strange\" | **undiagnosed — look before changing** |\n| 3 | A card is hard to click | probably a consequence of 1, **confirm it** |\n\n# 1. The loop — three correct behaviours composing into a wrong one\n\n1. `CardGrid::on_event_capture` calls `follow_hover` — *\"pointing at a card moves the cursor onto\n   it, so the mouse and the keyboard share one position\"*\n   (`heca-grid-ui/src/widgets/card_grid.rs:361`).\n2. The card under the cursor reports `wants_visible` (`Row`'s nav cursor, `docs/widgets.md` →\n   \"Following the cursor\").\n3. The exposé's row is a `ScrollRegion` with `.reveal_align(RevealAlign::Center)` and\n   `.overscroll(true)` (`heca/src/chrome/expose.rs`), so it centres whatever wants to be visible.\n\nSo hovering a card **scrolls that card to the centre of the row**, which moves it out from under the\npointer — and with a single workspace the row is the whole screen, so the slide is large and the card\nends up far from the mouse. It can then land under a *different* card and slide again.\n\n## The rule this breaks\n\n**A reveal exists to bring something into view that the user cannot see.** A card under the pointer is\nby definition visible — the user is pointing at it. Scrolling it is answering a question nobody asked,\nand it fights the input that caused it. Keyboard navigation is the opposite case: the cursor moves\nsomewhere possibly off-screen, so the region must follow it.\n\nSo the reveal must be **keyboard-driven only**. This is a framework rule, not an exposé one: any list\nor grid that follows its cursor has the same loop waiting (the sidebar is only saved by its rows being\nshort and its scroll being vertical).\n\n## Where to fix it — in `heca-grid-ui`, not in the exposé\n\nDo **not** special-case the map. `CardGrid` already knows which input moved the cursor: `follow_hover`\nis called from the pointer path, `step` / `step_vertical` from the intent path. The cursor's *position*\nis one value, but \"should the region come to it\" is a property of **how it moved**.\n\nOptions, in preference order:\n\n1. **The card asks to be revealed only when the cursor arrived by keyboard.** `wants_visible` is the\n   existing hook (`Component::wants_visible`), so the grid publishes a \"revealable\" signal alongside\n   the nav signal, and `follow_hover` moves the cursor **without** setting it. One extra signal on\n   `GridCell`, no new concept, and every `ScrollRegion` behaves for free.\n2. A `CardGrid::reveal_on_hover(bool)` builder — smaller, but it is a rule the *caller* has to\n   remember, which `AGENTS.md` says belongs in the widget.\n\nWhichever is chosen, `docs/widgets.md` → \"Following the cursor\" must say it: the reveal follows the\n**keyboard** cursor, and pointing at something never scrolls it.\n\n# 2. The animation — undiagnosed, look before changing\n\n\"Strange\" is a symptom, not a diagnosis, and there are at least four candidates. **Instrument before\nfixing** (the lesson that cost most of 2026-08-11: three speculative fixes, all plausible, two real\ndefects, and the app still broken until one traced run found the cause):\n\n- the scroll animation **restarting** every time the cursor moves one card, so it never settles and\n  each hover interrupts the last;\n- the reveal and the hover fighting — 1 above makes the map chase the pointer, which reads as\n  momentum rather than as a slide;\n- `RevealAlign::Center` + `.overscroll(true)` letting the row travel past its own ends, so the\n  motion has no natural stop;\n- the cursor card's own highlight/scale transition (`Row`'s cursor visuals) being timed differently\n  from the scroll, so two animations run at different rates on the same gesture.\n\nFixing 1 may well remove the strangeness entirely — **check whether it does before touching any\nanimation**, and if it does, say so rather than tuning something that no longer moves.\n\n# 3. Hard to click — confirm the cause\n\nMost likely a straight consequence of 1: the card slides out from under the pointer between the\nmouse arriving and the button going down, so the click lands on a neighbour or on nothing. If it\nstill happens with the reveal fixed, it is a separate defect and gets its own diagnosis — look at\nthe press/release pairing over an animating surface (a press and release on *different* targets is\nnot a click), and at whether the card's hit bounds track the animated position (`T400`'s open half:\n`bounds === what is drawn === what is clickable`, with `hit_bounds` still present).\n\n# Edge cases\n\n- The **wheel** must keep working over the map while hovering (the region already remembers the\n  target's natural position so it does not fight a manual scroll — do not regress that).\n- Clicking a card still activates it; only the reveal changes.\n- With the cursor moved by keyboard *while* the pointer rests on another card, the keyboard must win —\n  it is the one that needs the reveal.\n\n# Verification (Antonio drives — all three symptoms)\n\nOne workspace, several columns.\n\n1. Move the mouse across the cards: **the map does not move.**\n2. Watch the motion while arrowing: it starts and settles once, and does not restart or overshoot.\n3. Click a card the pointer is resting on: it activates, first time.\n4. Arrow with `ctrl+h/l` off the visible edge: the map scrolls to bring the cursor card into view.\n5. Wheel over the map: it scrolls and stays where it is put.",
      "notes": "",
      "statusLog": [
        {
          "id": "dcb1fdd2-065e-4632-a80b-19c1c7835e85-check-001-planned-in-progress",
          "date": "2026-08-12T07:03:13.738Z",
          "fromStatus": "planned",
          "toStatus": "in-progress",
          "title": "Started 2026-08-12 on Antonio's instruction, straight after T416 landed and he re-drove the exposé. It is a review of three symptoms, not one fix — see the description's table.",
          "description": "Started 2026-08-12 on Antonio's instruction, straight after T416 landed and he re-drove the exposé. It is a review of three symptoms, not one fix — see the description's table."
        }
      ],
      "decisions": [],
      "acceptedDecisions": [],
      "checklist": [],
      "subtasks": [],
      "dependsOn": [],
      "startedAt": "2026-08-12T07:03:13.738Z",
      "completedAt": "",
      "createdAt": "2026-08-11T22:55:42.451Z",
