    {
      "id": "7028cfd0-d674-4d51-ac5c-97213108ca6c",
      "phaseId": "5464ff74-fa17-4412-abaa-95aa19810b33",
      "number": 419,
      "shortId": "2XX4S",
      "priority": 8,
      "shortName": "the-expos-fits-everything-on-s",
      "title": "The exposé fits everything on screen — the zoom is computed, not configured",
      "status": "planned",
      "description": "**Antonio, 2026-08-12:** *\"is it right that some cards are shown outside the view? shouldn't we zoom out the whole area and the pane increases so that we can see them all at sight?\"* — and, on the shared-zoom question: *\"Ok\"*.\n\n# The defect\n\nAn exposé shows everything at once; that is what the name means. Today the map uses a **fixed** zoom (`[settings] overview_scale`, default `0.5`, read at `heca/src/chrome/expose.rs:363` as `geometry.overview_scale`) and scrolls whatever does not fit. So on a session wider than two screens the cards run off the edge and the surface stops being a map of the session and becomes a window onto part of one.\n\n# What to build — one shared zoom, fit to the view, clamped at both ends\n\n**One zoom for the whole map**, not one per row: Antonio chose this. It keeps the workspaces comparable in size so the map reads as one picture, where a per-row fit makes a two-pane workspace's cards huge beside a ten-pane one.\n\nThe zoom becomes computed, with `overview_scale` demoted from *the* zoom to a **maximum**:\n\n```\nfit   = min(fit_horizontal, fit_vertical)\nzoom  = min(fit, overview_scale).max(floor)\n```\n\n- **`fit_horizontal`** — the widest row must fit. Everything needed is already on the model\n  (`heca/src/chrome/expose.rs:80` `ExposeWorkspace`): `strip_width` is the sum of the columns\n  (already resolved by the layout, never recomputed), and `viewport` is `(x, width)` — the screen.\n  ⚠️ **Floats can stick out past `strip_width`**: `ExposeFloating` carries its own `x`/`w`, so the\n  row's true extent is `max(strip_width, max(float.x + float.w))`.\n- **`fit_vertical`** — N rows plus the gaps between them must fit. The gap is\n  `viewport_h * overview_gap * zoom` (niri's tenth-of-a-screen, `expose.rs:444`), so the height is\n  `zoom * viewport_h * (N + (N-1) * overview_gap)` and the fit falls out as\n  `1 / (N + (N-1) * overview_gap)` — independent of the pixel height, which is the tell that it is\n  the right form.\n- **`floor`** — a card must stay legible. A *scale* floor does not guarantee that, because columns\n  differ in width; the honest floor is a **minimum card width in logical pixels**, so\n  `floor = min_card_width / narrowest_column_width`. Under it the map overflows and scrolls again,\n  which is the only honest answer for thirty panes.\n\n⚠️ **`min` then `max`, never `clamp`** — `f64::clamp` panics when `min > max`, and a user with a\nsmall `overview_scale` and a large minimum card width is exactly that case. Legibility wins there;\nsay so in the doc comment.\n\n# The setting\n\n`overview_min_card_width` beside the other three in `LayoutOptions`\n(`heca-core/src/layout/types.rs:183`, where `overview_scale` / `overview_zoom_from` / `overview_gap`\nalready live), fed from `heca-config/src/settings.rs` like its neighbours.\n\n**AGENTS § 2**: a new `SettingsConfig` field that is not written into `config.default.toml` is a bug\n— the default then lives only in code, invisible to the user. Add it there with a comment, and to\n`README.md` beside the other overview settings. `overview_scale`'s own documentation has to change\ntoo: it is now a **maximum**, not the zoom.\n\n# What falls out for free\n\nIf nothing overflows, nothing scrolls — so the autocentring problem T418 addresses stops existing\nin the common case rather than being suppressed. Do **not** treat that as a reason to revert T418:\na session past the floor still scrolls, and the hover loop would still be there waiting.\n\n# Edge cases\n\n- **One workspace, one pane** — `fit` is large, `overview_scale` caps it, so the map does not blow\n  a single pane up to fill the screen.\n- **An empty workspace** — `strip_width` falls back to the viewport (`expose.rs:94`), so a row of\n  nothing is still a screen-shaped row and the fit is not divided by zero.\n- **A workspace with no columns but floats** — the extent must still come out positive.\n- The **zoom animation** on open (`set_zoom`, `overview_zoom_from`) multiplies whatever this\n  resolves to; it is a separate transform and needs no change.\n- `heca/src/chrome/expose.rs:995` and `:1156` assert card sizes against\n  `LayoutOptions::default().overview_scale` — they will need the computed zoom instead, and that is\n  the point: a test that hardcodes the old constant would pass while the map fits nothing.\n\n# Verification (Antonio drives)\n\n- Several workspaces, one wide with many columns: **every card is on screen**, and the widest row\n  just fits.\n- A small session: cards do not balloon — `overview_scale` still caps it.\n- A session past the floor: cards stay legible and the map scrolls, rather than shrinking to\n  slivers.\n- Reload (`prefix+Shift+r`) after changing `overview_min_card_width` in `config.toml` takes effect.",
      "notes": "",
