    {
      "id": "e139107d-dc27-4e5d-84b7-0d8adc9393d3",
      "phaseId": "5464ff74-fa17-4412-abaa-95aa19810b33",
      "number": 420,
      "shortId": "GDRND",
      "priority": 9,
      "shortName": "the-expos-fits-against-the-wro",
      "title": "Split the exposé into components — ExposeGrid / WorkspaceRow / ColumnCard / PaneCard, sized by shares",
      "status": "planned",
      "description": "**Antonio, 2026-08-12:** *\"I want you to reason in terms of Components that encapsulate their internal logic and are reusable. Like in React but write code Flutter/SwiftUI style.\"* — *\"we need to split the expose in components. ExposeGrid, WorkspaceRow, ColumnCard, PaneCard and align them using Flex or Grid. Each component encapsulates its internal business logic, accepts properties, events and callbacks.\"*\n\n⚠️ **The full context, the seven failed attempts and the state of the tree are in the P082 handoff** (`planner-handoff-show P082(F003)`). Read it first.\n\n# The problem\n\n`chrome/expose.rs::map()` is a ~500-line function that computes a picture **in pixels**: it derives a `zoom` and emits `Length::Px(col.width * zoom)`, `Px(row_h)`, `margin_left(float.x * zoom)`. Owning the pixels means owning every term — window size, overlay margin, panel padding, card gaps, row count, per-row height, the scroll region's centring, the overscroll range. Seven attempts, six wrong, each missing a *different* term. `AGENTS.md` § 0 names the tell exactly. And because no component exists, nothing has a headless test, so the only way to check it was Antonio photographing the app.\n\n# Layout\n\nBeside the surface — Antonio: *\"they should all be inside the chrome/expose folder since they are specific for that component.\"*\n\n```\nheca/src/chrome/expose/\n├── mod.rs             host wiring: register(), the layer, the emitter\n├── model.rs           ExposeWorkspace / ExposeColumn / ExposePane / ExposeFloating\n├── expose_grid.rs     rows stacked, sharing height by each ws's viewport_h; wraps CardGrid\n├── workspace_row.rs   columns sharing width; floats at their fractional rect\n├── column_card.rs     panes stacked, sharing the column's height\n└── pane_card.rs       name, active/cursor state, delete keys, activate\n```\n\nRule: **a component lives beside its surface, and moves up to `heca/src/components/` when a SECOND surface needs it.** ⚠️ Diverges from `AGENTS.md` § 0b — **add the qualification there as part of this work** or the next agent reverts it. `P087(F011)/T373` remains the home for the shared case and the headless-test pattern.\n\nEach takes properties, events and callbacks; **never `&AppState`** (§ 0b), so each is testable headless.\n\n# The one sizing rule: every size is a share, nothing is a pixel\n\n- column width — `Length::Pct(col.width / widest)`, `widest` = the widest row **in the map**, never its own row\n- pane height — its share of its column (`share()`: zero base + `grow` + shrink)\n- row height — its share of the total (`grow(ws.viewport_h)`, zero base)\n- rows `justify(Center)` so a short row sits centred\n\n⚠️ **`grow` alone will not do, and that is NOT a bug in `grow`.** `flex-grow` distributes free space, so children always fill their container — two columns would spread across the whole map and look like six. Standard CSS/taffy; do not \"fix\" it. A share of the *widest* row is a percentage against one shared denominator. `expose.rs:701` records the same trap from the other side.\n\n**Flex, not Grid**: `Grid` earns its keep when rows share a track structure; here each workspace has a different number of columns at different widths.\n\n# The zoom EFFECT stays — it is not the fit scale\n\n- **Animation** — `effects::Zoom` + `LayerRegistry::set_zoom(id, seconds, from)`, `PaintCx::with_scale` underneath (scales geometry *and* every pixel-measured value). Already generic for any layer including a plugin's. Antonio: *\"zoom effect should be a reusable api applicable to overlay\"* — it already is. **Untouched.**\n- **`overview_zoom` / `overview_scale`** — the fit scale. **This is what goes.** `overview_zoom_from` belongs to the animation and stays.\n\n# Already done and green — do not redo\n\n**Percentage margins** in `heca-grid-ui`: `margin_left/right/top/bottom` take `Length`, `impl From<f32> for Length` keeps every call site, taffy gets `length()`/`percent()`/`Auto`, and `LayoutEngine` resolves a root percentage margin against the space given. This is what lets a **float** — the one thing that cannot be a share of its parent's main axis — sit at `margin_left(Pct(float.x / widest))` with no pixel scale anywhere.\n\n# Delete\n\n`fit_zoom`, `overflows`, `overview_min_card_width` (`LayoutOptions` + `SettingsConfig` + `config.default.toml`), both `ScrollRegion`s in the map, and the three debug traces (`expose fit:`, `expose laid out:`, `center_pad:`).\n\n# Keep\n\nThe model, `CardGrid`, the `[[keys.surface]]` delete keys, the intents, the confirm, `pane_nav_key`, and the uncommitted T418 / overscroll / `center_pad` / resize fixes.\n\n# Order — each step leaves the map working and adds a test\n\n1. `P087(F011)/T373` — the `components/` contract + headless-test pattern to follow.\n2. `PaneCard` + test; swap the inline `Row::new()` card to it.\n3. `ColumnCard`, then `WorkspaceRow`, each replacing its inline equivalent.\n4. `ExposeGrid` last — and **this** is the commit where the fit arithmetic and the scroll regions come out, because it is the point at which nothing computes a pixel.\n\n# Tests — one per component, and the one that matters\n\n- `PaneCard` — renders its name, reports nav state, answers its delete keys\n- `ColumnCard` — panes in proportion; the stack never exceeds its box\n- `WorkspaceRow` — columns in proportion; a float lands at its fractional rect\n- **`ExposeGrid` — the whole map never exceeds the box it is given**, at several window sizes and row counts, including rows of unequal height\n\nThat last one would have caught every one of the seven failures.\n\n# Verification (Antonio drives)\n\nThree workspaces, ~26 panes: everything on screen at once, margins that look deliberate, the wheel moves nothing, resizing re-fits live, clicking a card the pointer rests on activates first time, and the open/close zoom still plays.",
      "notes": "",
      "statusLog": [],
      "decisions": [],
      "acceptedDecisions": [],
      "checklist": [],
