  "createdAt": "2026-07-26T11:28:31.721Z",
  "updatedAt": "2026-08-12T21:26:16.294Z",
  "handoff": "# P082 handoff — 2026-08-12 · the exposé is rebuilt as COMPONENTS; the pixel/zoom layer is the bug\n\n## 0. READ BEFORE WRITING A LINE\n\nRead the parent FEATURE (F003) and PHASE (P082) descriptions first. Then `AGENTS.md` § 0 (the\nPRE-FLIGHT), § 0b (widget vs component), § 0c (events are DOM-shaped), ⭐⭐ RULE ZERO, and\n`docs/surface-compositor.md` §§2/3/5/6.\n\n**Antonio, 2026-08-12, after a day of failed fixes:**\n*\"If you have to redo all it means we are writing bad code. Hard coding widget positioning, painting\nin the expose instead of having proper components such as a PaneRow that can be reused in a grid, a\nflex or anywhere. The rule is always to write reusable components, encapsulate business logic in\ncomponents and widgets.\"*\n*\"I want you to reason in terms of Components that encapsulate their internal logic and are\nreusable. Like in React but write code Flutter/SwiftUI style.\"*\n*\"Stop inventing things.\"*\n\n## 1. THE DIAGNOSIS — why everything below happened\n\n`heca/src/chrome/expose.rs::map()` is a ~500-line function that **computes a picture in pixels**. It\nderives a `zoom` from the session extents and the window, then emits absolute sizes:\n`Length::Px(col.width * zoom)`, `Px(row_h)`, `margin_left(float.x * zoom)`.\n\nThe moment it does that it has taken over the layout engine's job — and then it owns *every* term:\nthe window size, the overlay margin, the panel padding, the gaps between cards, the row count, each\nrow's height, the scroll region's centring, the overscroll range. **Miss one term and the whole map\nis wrong by exactly that term.** That is what happened, five times, each time a different term.\n\n`AGENTS.md` § 0 names this exact tell: *\"you are writing a paint, a measure, a hit-test, a scroll\noffset… Every one of those is some existing widget's job.\"*\n\n**The second cost is worse than the first.** There is no `PaneCard`, no `ColumnCard`, no\n`WorkspaceRow` — so nothing has a headless test, and the ONLY way to check any of it was Antonio\nopening the app and photographing it. He was the debugger for seven rounds.\n\n**The zoom should not exist.** \"How big must this be so everything fits\" is the layout engine's\nwhole purpose. As *ratios* — a column is `width / widest`, a pane is `height / column`, a row is\n`viewport_h / total` — taffy answers it exactly, at every window size, with no term to forget.\n\n## 2. WHAT TO BUILD — components, Flutter/SwiftUI style\n\nSplit the exposé into components, each encapsulating its own logic, taking properties, events and\ncallbacks. Names agreed with Antonio:\n\n| component | what it is | owns |\n|---|---|---|\n| `PaneCard` | one pane as a card | its name, active/cursor state, its own key handlers (delete keys), its activate |\n| `ColumnCard` | a column: panes stacked, sharing the column's height by their real heights | the vertical share; nothing about zoom |\n| `WorkspaceRow` | a row: columns sharing width by their real widths, plus floats at their own fractional rect | the horizontal share, the float placement |\n| `ExposeGrid` | the rows, stacked, sharing height by each workspace's `viewport_h` | the row share, the cursor (wraps/uses `CardGrid`) |\n\n### Where they live — Antonio, 2026-08-12\n\n**Next to the surface, not in `heca/src/components/`**: *\"I think column_card, pane_card,\nworkspace_row and expose_grid should all be inside the chrome/expose folder since they are specific\nfor that component.\"*\n\n```\nheca/src/chrome/expose/\n├── mod.rs             host wiring: register(), the layer, the emitter\n├── model.rs           ExposeWorkspace / ExposeColumn / ExposePane / ExposeFloating\n├── expose_grid.rs\n├── workspace_row.rs\n├── column_card.rs\n└── pane_card.rs\n```\n\nThe rule: **a component lives beside its surface, and moves up to `heca/src/components/` the day a\nSECOND surface needs it** — DRY applied when the duplication is real rather than predicted.\n\n⚠️ This diverges from `AGENTS.md` § 0b, which says components live in `heca/src/components/` full\nstop. **Add the qualification to § 0b as part of this work**, or the next agent will \"correct\" it\nback. `P087(F011)/T373` (the `components/` directory + its test pattern) is still the right home for\nthe shared case and for the headless-test pattern these should follow.\n\n### The zoom EFFECT is not the fit scale — do not delete it\n\nTwo different things are called zoom, and only one is going:\n\n- **The open/close animation** — `effects::Zoom` (`heca-grid-ui`) + `LayerRegistry::set_zoom(id,\n  seconds, from)`, with `PaintCx::with_scale` underneath (which scales geometry *and* every\n  pixel-measured value: font size, radius, border width, glow falloff). Already generic — **any**\n  layer, including a plugin's, declares how it arrives and leaves. Antonio, 2026-08-12: *\"zoom effect\n  should be a reusable api applicable to overlay… a separate reusable thing that expose can use\"* —\n  it already is. **Untouched by this work.**\n- **`overview_zoom` / `overview_scale`** — the *fit* scale. This is what goes.\n\n`overview_zoom_from` belongs to the animation and stays.\n\nAligned with **`Flex`** — not `Grid`. Antonio asked why; the answer is that `Grid` earns its keep\nwhen rows share a **track structure**, and here every workspace has a different number of columns at\ndifferent widths, so `Grid` would be `Flex` wearing a costume.\n\n### The one sizing rule\n\n**Every size is a share; nothing is a pixel.**\n\n- a column's width is `Length::Pct(col.width / widest)` where `widest` is the **widest row in the\n  whole map**, not its own row;\n- a pane's height is its share of its column (`share()` already does this: zero base + `grow` +\n  shrink);\n- a row's height is its share of the total (`grow(ws.viewport_h)` with a zero base);\n- rows use `justify(Center)` so a short row sits in the middle.\n\n⚠️ **`grow` alone will NOT work, and this is not a bug in `grow`.** `flex-grow` distributes *free\nspace*, so children always fill their container — a workspace of two columns would spread them\nacross the whole map and look identical to one holding six. That is standard CSS/taffy behaviour and\nmust not be \"fixed\"; AGENTS' own rule is to prefer common, well-known APIs. \"A share of the widest\nrow\" is genuinely a **percentage**, against a single shared denominator. The existing comment at\n`expose.rs:701` records this same trap from the other direction.\n\n### Already built and green — do not redo\n\n**Percentage margins** (`heca-grid-ui`, done 2026-08-12): `Style::margin_left/right/top/bottom` are\n`Option<Length>` instead of `Option<f32>`, `impl From<f32> for Length` keeps every existing call site\nworking, the taffy conversion maps `Px`→`length()`, `Pct`→`percent()`, `Auto`→`Auto`, and\n`LayoutEngine` resolves a percentage margin on the **root** against the space it was given.\n\nThis exists because a **float** is the one thing that cannot be a share of its parent's main axis —\nit is placed at its own rect. With `Pct` margins it becomes\n`margin_left(Pct(float.x / widest))` / `margin_top(Pct(float.y / viewport_h))`, so B works with no\npixel scale anywhere. Antonio picked this over dropping float placement or keeping a scale for\nfloats alone.\n\n### Delete as part of the work\n\n`fit_zoom`, `overflows`, `overview_min_card_width` (config + `LayoutOptions` + `config.default.toml`),\nboth `ScrollRegion`s in the map (nothing overflows once the engine fits it), and the three debug\ntraces (`expose fit:`, `expose laid out:`, `center_pad:`). Re-check whether `overview_zoom` still\nmeans anything — under shares there is no zoom to cap.\n\n### Keep\n\n`ExposeWorkspace`/`ExposeColumn`/`ExposePane`/`ExposeFloating` (the model), `CardGrid` (the cursor),\nthe delete keys via `[[keys.surface]]`, the intents, the confirm, `pane_nav_key`.\n\n## 3. THE TESTS THAT MUST COME WITH IT\n\nThe absence of these is why Antonio had to debug this by eye. Each component gets a headless test:\n\n- `PaneCard` — renders its name, reports its nav state, answers its delete keys.\n- `ColumnCard` — panes get height in proportion to their weights, and the stack never exceeds its box.\n- `WorkspaceRow` — columns in proportion to the shared denominator; a float lands at its fractional rect.\n- `ExposeGrid` — rows in proportion; **the whole map never exceeds the box it is given, at several\n  window sizes and row counts**, including rows of unequal height.\n\nThat last one is the single test that would have caught every failure below.\n\n## 4. WHAT WAS TRIED AND FAILED — do not repeat any of these\n\nSeven attempts, six wrong, all of them corrections to arithmetic that should not exist:\n\n1. **Fit against the workspace viewport** — wrong: the overlay covers the whole window. (`screen_w=664`\n   on a 995px window.)\n2. **Fit against the window** — overshot: ignored the gaps between columns and the panel padding.\n3. **Subtract padding + column gaps** — still overshot: missed `Overlay::VIEWPORT_MARGIN` (24px each\n   side, a constant in *another crate*). Found only by the geometry trace.\n4. **Sum the row heights instead of first × N** — changed nothing; the rows were equal after all.\n   `fit_h` was identical before and after.\n5. **Slack in the fit / epsilon in the overflow test** — the fit landed on the boundary exactly\n   (`727` in `730`), and the region flipped to its overflow branch on rounding.\n6. **\"Nothing asks to be revealed while it fits\"** — the region *still* shifted the content (`@35,213`).\n7. **Overscroll bounds collapse when content fits** — this one is CORRECT and is kept (see §5).\n\n**The lesson, twice learned today and recorded in memory:** measuring named the cause in one round\neach time; predicting from a screenshot was wrong six times. Instrument first.\n\n## 5. STATE OF THE TREE\n\nBranch `feat/palette-modes`. **One commit landed** — `9673be9` (T416, surface-scoped keybindings).\n**Everything after it is UNCOMMITTED and green**: whole workspace tests pass, clippy 0 warnings with\n`--all-features`.\n\nUncommitted work, all of which is worth keeping:\n\n- **T418 (hover)** — `CardGrid::reveal_state()` + `Row::reveal_when(Signal<bool>)`: the reveal follows\n  the keyboard, never the pointer. Driven by Antonio: *\"now is better\"*. The animation and the\n  hard-to-click symptoms were never separately confirmed.\n- **`ScrollRegion` overscroll bounds** — collapse to `(0,0)` on an axis whose content fits. This\n  **reverses a deliberate earlier decision** (the old test\n  `a_wheel_moves_an_overscrolling_region_even_when_its_content_fits`, whose rationale was that\n  clamping left the surface \"frozen under the mouse\"). Antonio reversed it: *\"mouse wheel can still\n  scroll even if there's no need and put card off the screen\"*. The reversal and its reason are\n  written into the test.\n- **`center_pad`** — centres the *content* when it fits, the cursor only when it overflows.\n- **`refresh_visible_layers` on `MutationKind::Config`** — so a window resize rebuilds the map.\n- **Percentage margins** (§2).\n- **The three debug traces** — remove them with the rewrite.\n\n## 6. STILL OPEN, NOT STARTED\n\n- **T421 — the lag.** 26 panes across 3 workspaces makes the map \"very very laggy\", worst on\n  open/close. Prime suspect, unmeasured: `layout_layers` (`chrome/mod.rs`) runs a full\n  `LayoutEngine::compute` over every visible layer **every frame**, with no dirty check —\n  `heca-grid-ui` has no layout-dirty concept at all. Second suspect: the frosted backdrop's\n  full-screen blur pass per animation frame. **Measure before touching either.**\n- **T420 — this rewrite.** The margins Antonio kept flagging are partly inherent to one shared zoom\n  (whichever axis binds leaves slack on the other) and partly the fit being wrong; under shares the\n  question disappears.\n- **The workspace indicator.** Antonio approved a **left rail, always visible**: one mark per\n  workspace, each a small bar whose height is proportional to its row, filled for the one the cursor\n  is in, name on the active one. It is a NEW generic `heca-grid-ui` widget (nothing existing fits —\n  `MarkerGroup` is one group's bar, `RailCell` is a square icon cell, `ScrollBar` is bound to a\n  scroll offset). **Unanswered: should a mark be clickable to jump to that workspace?**\n- **T327's exit sequencing** (fade over shrink) — still waiting on a layer's lifetime following the\n  whole exit.\n\n## 7. HOW ANTONIO WORKS\n\n- **He drives every visual check.** Green tests found none of this.\n- **Components, always.** Encapsulate logic, take props/events/callbacks, reusable anywhere.\n- **Plain English, short. Answer, don't narrate.** He said \"too much hassle\" to a long options list.\n- **Never mention the context budget** — pacing is his call.\n- **Measure before fixing.** Six wrong guesses in one day is the proof.\n- **Never commit unless asked.**",
